                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "58b77d50-f83c-3514-9190-a334d62e5e04",
                        "entry_id": "cd28bc75-1ccf-33f4-b2f9-738a9f35d5b3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Linked or fused to a structure of a type different from itself; for example, attachment of a stamen to a petal is adnate, while attachment of a stamen to another stamen is connate."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "78967ccc-78b4-4e72-bed2-8d0f1b6e0b86",
                                "sense_id": "58b77d50-f83c-3514-9190-a334d62e5e04",
                                "text": "Adnate mushroom gills are broadly attached to the stalk slightly above the bottom of the gill, with most of the gill fused to the stem."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2ca97998-8aed-4be9-a8be-661681824e9e",
                                "sense_id": "58b77d50-f83c-3514-9190-a334d62e5e04",
                                "text": "An anther is adnate when fixed by its whole length to the filament."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02dc7da7-97ed-4cb7-a7d6-0fc5a9de0012",
                                "sense_id": "58b77d50-f83c-3514-9190-a334d62e5e04",
                                "text": "The ovary is more adnate to the calyx than in any other species of the genus."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cd2209c8-4eaa-4830-9b6b-83b8b776c705",
                                "sense_id": "58b77d50-f83c-3514-9190-a334d62e5e04",
                                "text": "Morphologically and chemically X. isidiigera is also similar to X. australasica, but the isidia are typically thinner and more coralloid branched and the thallus more adnate in the latter species."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5100088f-f85d-4abf-a59d-9b71861ad9a9",
                                "sense_id": "58b77d50-f83c-3514-9190-a334d62e5e04",
                                "text": "Morphologically, Hypotrachyna kriegeri closely resembles more adnate morphotypes of H. imbricatula."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ffcf369c-9b72-302b-be6a-653a0acd5750",
                        "entry_id": "cd28bc75-1ccf-33f4-b2f9-738a9f35d5b3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Growing with one side adherent to a stem; applied to the lateral zooids of corals and other compound animals. in fish, having the eyes fused and unable to rotate independently"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "62d40dd1-d331-4347-aeaa-2235aac6b932",
                                "sense_id": "ffcf369c-9b72-302b-be6a-653a0acd5750",
                                "text": "It differs from N. eleutherus by possessing a more adnate adipose fin and more prominent saddles and from N. albater by having 8 soft pectoral rays, a submarginal adipose bar, and no prominent basicaudal bar (Douglas 1972)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
